    "gold_solution": "```python\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"JSSP\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_jobs = 4\n    n_machines = 4\n    processing_times = [[25, 22, 8, 14], [16, 25, 30, 23], [27, 11, 7, 9], [22, 11, 20, 19]]\n    machine_assignments = [[1, 2, 0, 3], [1, 3, 2, 0], [2, 1, 0, 3], [1, 2, 3, 0]]\n\n    # Big-M: sum of all processing times\n    M = sum(processing_times[j][o] for j in range(n_jobs) for o in range(n_machines))\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Start times for each operation\n    S = {}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            S[(j,o)] = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Start_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # Makespan variable\n    Cmax = m.addVar(lb=0, ub=M, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=\"Cmax\")\n\n    # Build machine -> [(job, op)] mapping\n    machine_ops = {mach: [] for mach in range(n_machines)}\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines):\n            machine_ops[machine_assignments[j][o]].append((j, o))\n\n    # Disjunctive binary variables for each pair on same machine\n    Y = {}\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Y_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # --- Objective: minimize makespan ---\n    m.setObjective(Cmax, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Precedence: operations within a job must follow order\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        for o in range(n_machines - 1):\n            m.addConstr(\n                S[(j,o+1)] >= S[(j,o)] + processing_times[j][o],\n                name=f\"Prec_{j}_{o}\")\n\n    # 2. Disjunctive: no two operations on the same machine overlap\n    for mach in range(n_machines):\n        ops = machine_ops[mach]\n        for i in range(len(ops)):\n            for k in range(i+1, len(ops)):\n                j1, o1 = ops[i]\n                j2, o2 = ops[k]\n                p1 = processing_times[j1][o1]\n                p2 = processing_times[j2][o2]\n                y = Y[(j1,o1,j2,o2)]\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j1,o1)] + p1 <= S[(j2,o2)] + M * (1 - y),\n                    name=f\"Disj_A_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n                m.addConstr(\n                    S[(j2,o2)] + p2 <= S[(j1,o1)] + M * y,\n                    name=f\"Disj_B_{j1}_{o1}_{j2}_{o2}\")\n\n    # 3. Makespan: Cmax >= completion of last operation of each job\n    for j in range(n_jobs):\n        last_op = n_machines - 1\n        m.addConstr(\n            Cmax >= S[(j, last_op)] + processing_times[j][last_op],\n            name=f\"Makespan_J{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Memo\n\nTo: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Job Shop Scheduling Challenge for the Upcoming Production Window\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized workstations that support a variety of product builds. For the next production window, we need to coordinate a defined set of customer orders through these workstations. Each order consists of a series of operations that must be performed in a fixed sequence. Every operation is tied to a designated machine and has a known processing duration. Because the same equipment is shared across different orders, there is inherent competition for machine time that must be resolved through careful scheduling.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe task is to assign start times and machine slots for all operations so that equipment is utilized efficiently while honoring each job\u2019s prescribed route. Processing times vary across operations, which creates uneven loading and potential bottlenecks on certain machines. If we sequence poorly, we risk unnecessary idle time, work-in-process buildup, and a longer overall production cycle. Conversely, an effective schedule will stage long and short runs to smooth machine utilization, reduce waiting between steps, and deliver a timely finish for the entire set of jobs.\n\nKey Requirements and Rules\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can handle only one operation at a time; no overlaps or double-booking.\n- Fixed job sequence: Operations within the same job must be executed in the specified order; no resequencing.\n- Designated resources: Each operation must be performed on its assigned machine only.\n- Known durations: Each operation has a defined processing time that must be fully allocated.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it must run to completion without interruption or transfer.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, end-to-end schedule that sequences all operations across the available machines to complete all jobs in the shortest possible total time. In practical terms, we aim to minimize the overall completion time (makespan) for the full set of jobs while adhering to all rules above.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: 4\n- Number of machines: 4\n- Machine assignments (job/operation \u2192 machine): Machine Assignments (which machine each operation uses):\n  Machine indices: 0 to 3\n  - Job 1: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 3]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 3, Op3: Machine 2, Op4: Machine 0]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: Machine 2, Op2: Machine 1, Op3: Machine 0, Op4: Machine 3]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: Machine 1, Op2: Machine 2, Op3: Machine 3, Op4: Machine 0]\n- Processing times (job/operation \u2192 duration): Processing Times (time units per operation):\n  Each job has 4 operations performed in sequence.\n  - Job 1: [Op1: 25, Op2: 22, Op3: 8, Op4: 14]\n  - Job 2: [Op1: 16, Op2: 25, Op3: 30, Op4: 23]\n  - Job 3: [Op1: 27, Op2: 11, Op3: 7, Op4: 9]\n  - Job 4: [Op1: 22, Op2: 11, Op3: 20, Op4: 19]",
    "LLM_description_template": "Memo\n\nTo: Plant Leadership and Production Planning Team\nFrom: Manufacturing Operations Manager\nSubject: Job Shop Scheduling Challenge for the Upcoming Production Window\n\nContext\nOur facility operates multiple specialized workstations that support a variety of product builds. For the next production window, we need to coordinate a defined set of customer orders through these workstations. Each order consists of a series of operations that must be performed in a fixed sequence. Every operation is tied to a designated machine and has a known processing duration. Because the same equipment is shared across different orders, there is inherent competition for machine time that must be resolved through careful scheduling.\n\nScheduling Challenge\nThe task is to assign start times and machine slots for all operations so that equipment is utilized efficiently while honoring each job\u2019s prescribed route. Processing times vary across operations, which creates uneven loading and potential bottlenecks on certain machines. If we sequence poorly, we risk unnecessary idle time, work-in-process buildup, and a longer overall production cycle. Conversely, an effective schedule will stage long and short runs to smooth machine utilization, reduce waiting between steps, and deliver a timely finish for the entire set of jobs.\n\nKey Requirements and Rules\n- Machine exclusivity: Each machine can handle only one operation at a time; no overlaps or double-booking.\n- Fixed job sequence: Operations within the same job must be executed in the specified order; no resequencing.\n- Designated resources: Each operation must be performed on its assigned machine only.\n- Known durations: Each operation has a defined processing time that must be fully allocated.\n- No preemption: Once an operation starts, it must run to completion without interruption or transfer.\n\nGoal\nBuild a feasible, end-to-end schedule that sequences all operations across the available machines to complete all jobs in the shortest possible total time. In practical terms, we aim to minimize the overall completion time (makespan) for the full set of jobs while adhering to all rules above.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Production Data\n- Number of jobs: {NUM_JOBS}\n- Number of machines: {NUM_MACHINES}\n- Machine assignments (job/operation \u2192 machine): {MACHINE_ASSIGNMENTS}\n- Processing times (job/operation \u2192 duration): {PROCESSING_TIMES}"
